  "summary": "The traditional silversmithing craft creating decorative and functional mate gourds and bombilla straws.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate repoussé and filigree silverwork on mate gourds and bombillas, featuring floral motifs, geometric patterns, and symbolic imagery. This craft evolved from indigenous and Spanish colonial techniques, becoming integral to the mate drinking ceremony and a symbol of cultural identity throughout the Southern Cone.",
  "culturalContext": "Mate was first consumed by the indigenous Guaraní who live in what is now Paraguay, southeastern Brazil, Argentina, Bolivia, Uruguay, and  the Tupí people who lived in neighbouring areas. Later, it spread to parts of southern Brazil and northeast Argentina, particularly areas that were Paraguayan territory before the Paraguayan War. Therefore, the scientific name of the yerba mate is Ilex paraguariensis.\n\nWith the Spanish colonization of Paraguay in the late 16th century the consumption of yerba mate spread to Spanish settlers, and in the 17th century to the Río de la Plata and from there to Peru and Chile. This widespread consumption turned it into Paraguay's main commodity above other wares such as tobacco, cotton and beef.",
